rough subtle calculated movements  rather than through brute force or  button mashing the pace of combat in its  initial stages seems clunky and  disorienting especially if you're used  to faster paced fighting games but once  you've grown accustomed to its tempo  it's the most enjoyable and fulfilling  quality for honor has to offer  aside from a few informational videos  for Honor's most useful training tool is  its single-player story mode it  functions is a long-form tutorial  putting you into various storage driven  scenarios that teach you the  fundamentals of combat  unfortunately the narrative that links  these scenarios together is a  nonsensical mess a warlord named  apollyon whose intention is to ensure an  eternal age of all at war instigates the  conflict gripping its world but her  motivation is so unclear and muddled  that she rarely makes for an  entertaining presence meanwhile the  battle hungry ensemble cast tasked with  either standing up to or supporting her  are marred by lackluster  characterization as they provide little  in the way of relatability  when you tackle for Honor's multiplayer  there are plenty of modes to dive into  however the most varied and entertaining  of the bunch is Dominion a 4v4 mode  where you and your team cooperate to  capture and hold three zones in a  battlefield with AI minion rushing from  points a point defending a zone are  working with your teammates to obtain  others is exhilarating combat in this  mode can become too chaotic at times  which often causes you and your  teammates to mindlessly button mash your  way to victory against the last standing  hero despite this Dominion encapsulate  the sensation of a large-scale medieval  battle on a smaller scale distilling the  desperation of a relentless charge and  the ruthless sword fights that ensue in  its wake  elimination mode meanwhile emphasizes  and amplifies the complexity of  forerunners team-based duels it's  uncomplicated in premise a 4v4 faced off  to the death with no response  combat is thrilling and challenging this  mode especially when it's up to you to  secure a victory against the full enemy  squad you come to understand not only  how to fight against multiple foes but  also how to judge when and where it's  appropriate to do so learning this is  that times punishing or unfair but when  your reflexes and awareness work in your  favor it's difficult not to feel an  overwhelming satisfaction in how the  game makes it possible to win against  all odds  this Dominion demonstrates for Honor's  capacity to create varied and exciting  moments elimination embodies the thrill  and depth of its team-based fights then  dual showcases combat at its most tense  in absolute this strictly one-on-one  battle mode forces you to rely on the  strength of your heroes based moveset  dual stripped down nature showcases the  brilliance of four honors one-on-one  combat elevating its other modes in the  process through how it condenses what  each has to offer into a raw and brief  competitive instance  after slaying countless foes it's clear  the impact for Honor's combat has had  its fundamental tenets of discipline and  restraint are bestowed upon you  permanently forever changing the way you  perceive a melee combat encounter in a  game in its highest moment it's  difficult to put down it's slow paced  combat and narrative shortcomings might  turn off those unwilling to take the  time to dive deep into what it has to  offer however make no mistake those who  do will be rewarded with some of the  most satisfying multiplayer melee  fighting conceived in recent years  [Music]
 

